354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E ( The application of unmanned aerial vehicles \(UAVs\) in precision agriculture has grown significantly over the past decade, driven by the need for scalable, high-resolution, and on-demand crop surveillance that satellite remote sensing cannot economically provide. Early UAV-based agricultural studies focused on multispectral imaging to compute vegetation indices such as NDVI \(Normalized Difference Vegetation Index\), which serves as a proxy for canopy health and photosynthetic activity. These initial deployments demonstrated that drone-derived NDVI maps could identify stressed crop zones at sub-meter resolution, enabling targeted intervention over specific field patches rather than broad-acre chemical treatment.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E ( Sa et al. [1] developed an early hybrid UAV platform combining a Pixhawk-based multirotor with onboard ROS processing and cloud offloading for fruit detection tasks, achieving 91% average precision. Their work established the viability of coupling the Pixhawk ecosystem with cloud inference but highlighted a critical limitation: heavy onboard GPU hardware increased UAV weight beyond the optimal endurance operating point for survey missions. This trade-off between computational capability and flight endurance is a persistent challenge in the literature that the AgriDrone platform resolves by fully offloading all AI inference to cloud infrastructure, keeping the airborne payload limited to the imaging and telemetry subsystems.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E ( Deng et al. [2] deployed a DJI Phantom 4 for real-time pest detection using YOLOv3, achieving 88% mAP at near-real-time inference speeds. Their study demonstrated the practical utility of YOLO-family detectors in aerial agricultural contexts but was constrained by the closed proprietary hardware of the DJI platform, which limits telemetry access, payload customization, and integration with open-source flight management software. The Pixhawk 2.4.8 flight controller employed in AgriDrone AI AI AI AI exposes full MAVLink telemetry over both serial and UDP, enabling the cloud backend to receive real-time position, altitude, battery, and attitude data, which is persisted in the Neon PostgreSQL sensor_data table alongside AI detection records.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (Zhang et al. [5] combined a multirotor UAV platform with distributed IoT soil sensors to achieve comprehensive crop stress monitoring, demonstrating that geospatially registered multi-modal data significantly improves the diagnostic precision of disease localization. Their system architecture, however, lacked a real-time cloud dashboard, relying instead on post-mission batch processing. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
